Done. I built **`/workspace/life-lab`** — a terminal cellular automaton playground. Because I genuinely find emergent behavior fascinating, and writing this was the kind of "something I want" that felt right for a blank workspace.

## What it is

A curses-based TUI that lets you play with **13 different outer-totalistic CA rules** on a toroidal grid — not just Conway's Game of Life, but a whole zoo of them (HighLife, Seeds, Day & Night, Diamoeba, Maze, Coral, Anneal, Replicator, 2x2, Move, Amoeba, LongLife). Each rule has wildly different behavior — some explode, some stabilize into crystals, some oscillate endlessly.

Cells are **age-colored** (the longer a cell survives, the further through a 7-color rainbow it drifts), so you can literally watch the "senior" cells in a stable glider vs. the "fresh" cells at the leading edge.

## Controls

| Key | Action |
|---|---|
| `space` | pause / resume |
| `n` | step one generation (when paused) |
| `s` / `S` | cycle rules forward / backward |
| `1`–`9`, `0` | stamp a pattern at the cursor (glider, LWSS, pulsar, r-pentomino, pentadecathlon, acorn, diehard, Gosper glider gun, block, beehive) |
| `arrows` | move cursor / pattern anchor |
| `r` | randomize · `c` · clear |
| `h` | help overlay · `q` · quit |

## How to run

```bash
./life-lab               # defaults: Life, 20fps, 28% density
./life-lab --rule Seeds --fps 30 --seed 42
./life-lab --rule Diamoeba --density 0.4
```

## One caveat worth flagging

It's a `curses` TUI — it needs a real terminal to render. I can't demo it live to you here since I'm in a headless shell. If you want, I can:

